\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces From three centerline points to the average curvature, on a made-up artery computed with the thesis code. At $P_i$, the points $B_i$ and $F_i$ lie 5\,mm in a straight line behind and ahead of it, and $\kappa _i$ follows from the area $A_i$ of their triangle and the lengths of its three sides, each shown in the colour of its term in the formula (left). Repeated at every centerline point, this gives the curvature profile, which has no value within about 5\,mm of either end of the artery (grey) (middle). The average curvature $T$ is the area under the profile divided by the length it covers, so a rectangle of height $T$ over the same stretch has the same area (right).\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method-curvature}{{6.4}{33}{From three centerline points to the average curvature, on a made-up artery computed with the thesis code. At $P_i$, the points $B_i$ and $F_i$ lie 5\,mm in a straight line behind and ahead of it, and $\kappa _i$ follows from the area $A_i$ of their triangle and the lengths of its three sides, each shown in the colour of its term in the formula (left). Repeated at every centerline point, this gives the curvature profile, which has no value within about 5\,mm of either end of the artery (grey) (middle). The average curvature $T$ is the area under the profile divided by the length it covers, so a rectangle of height $T$ over the same stretch has the same area (right).\relax }{figure.caption.21}{}}
\newlabel{fig:method-curvature@cref}{{[figure][4][6]6.4}{[1][31][]33}}
