\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces The test of the geometric features and the estimate of their added predictive value, shown with made-up numbers. The base model holds the covariates (grey) and the extended model adds the geometric features (coloured). The two are compared by a likelihood ratio test, and each association is reported as a hazard ratio per SD with its 95\% confidence interval (left). In the cross-validation, each column is one participant with the nine segments of the curvature model, and the model is fitted on nine folds and predicts the held-out fold, repeated 50 times (middle). The cross-validated risks of the two models are shown as back-to-back histograms and compared by the C-statistic, calibration, the Brier score and the log loss (right).\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method-evaluation}{{6.6}{40}{The test of the geometric features and the estimate of their added predictive value, shown with made-up numbers. The base model holds the covariates (grey) and the extended model adds the geometric features (coloured). The two are compared by a likelihood ratio test, and each association is reported as a hazard ratio per SD with its 95\% confidence interval (left). In the cross-validation, each column is one participant with the nine segments of the curvature model, and the model is fitted on nine folds and predicts the held-out fold, repeated 50 times (middle). The cross-validated risks of the two models are shown as back-to-back histograms and compared by the C-statistic, calibration, the Brier score and the log loss (right).\relax }{figure.caption.24}{}}
\newlabel{fig:method-evaluation@cref}{{[figure][6][6]6.6}{[1][40][]40}}
